      text No ARIA is better than Bad ARIA. Before using any ARIA, . @104,187,590,20
      link "Read this to understand why no ARIA is better than bad ARIA" /WAI/content-assets/wai-aria-practices/../../ARIA/apg/practices/read-me-first/ @104,219,590,21
    h2 About This Pattern @87,267,624,21
    text An is an element that displays a brief, important message in a way that attracts the user's attention without interrupting the user's task. Dynamically rendered alerts are automatically announced by most screen readers, and in some operating systems, they may trigger an alert sound. It is important to note that, at this time, screen readers do not inform users of alerts that are present on the page before page load completes. @87,300,624,102
    link alert https://w3c.github.io/aria/#alert @87,414,624,20
    text Because alerts are intended to provide important and potentially time-sensitive information without interfering with the user's ability to continue working, it is crucial they do not affect keyboard focus. The is designed for situations where interrupting work flow is necessary. @87,446,624,61
    link "Alert Dialog Pattern" ../alertdialog/ @87,519,624,20
    text It is also important to avoid designing alerts that disappear automatically. An alert that disappears too quickly can lead to failure to meet . Another critical design consideration is the frequency of interruption caused by alerts. Frequent interruptions inhibit usability for people with visual and cognitive disabilities, which makes meeting the requirements of more difficult. @87,551,624,81
    link "WCAG 2.0 success criterion 2.2.3" https://www.w3.org/TR/UNDERSTANDING-WCAG20/time-limits-no-exceptions.html @87,644,624,21
    link "WCAG 2.0 success criterion 2.2.4" https://www.w3.org/TR/UNDERSTANDING-WCAG20/time-limits-postponed.html @87,677,624,20
    card @87,709,624,83
